## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design end-to-end tests for multi-region, multi-tenant feature flags, covering UI, API, and background workers, with rapid toggling and race conditions. Demonstrates seeds, isolation strategies, and SLA verification.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flag precedence and propagation across layers
- Cross-region consistency (UI, API, workers)
- Concurrency, load testing, and idempotency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test scaffold for E2E feature-flag toggles
async function testFlagToggle(tenantId, flagKey, value) {
  await api.setFlag(tenantId, flagKey, value);
  await wait(50);
  const uiState = await ui.getFlagState(tenantId, flagKey);
  const workerState = await worker.getFlagState(tenantId, flagKey);
  if (uiState !== value || workerState !== value) throw new Error('Inconsistent state');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure cross-region propagation latency? 
- How do you handle partial failures (region down) and ensure eventual consistency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

